# candor-java self-gate (SPEC §7.12): the analyzer reads bytecode and writes reports — Fs/Env
# are its job. It must never reach the network, a database, or IPC — anywhere, ever.
#
# It spawns a subprocess in exactly ONE place: `candor verify`, the dynamic honesty oracle, which
# runs YOUR program under a -javaagent to check candor's static claim against reality. That Exec is
# the feature, not a hidden effect. Prefix scopes can't say "deny Exec everywhere except one
# sub-package", so `ci/self-gate.sh` proves the two halves separately:
#   (1) the WHOLE tool under THIS policy — no Net/Db/Ipc, with no package excluded;
#   (2) the Exec-performing methods are EXACTLY the two declared in that script
#       (`Candor.main`, `verify.VerifyCli.main`) — so a new subprocess anywhere fails the gate,
#       io.poly.candor.verify included.
#
# WHY `deny Net Db Ipc` AND NOT `deny Net Db Exec Ipc`. The stricter string used to live here, and CI
# made it pass by COPYING build/classes and DELETING io/poly/candor/verify first — carving the
# exemption out a whole PACKAGE at a time, so everything in it sat outside the Exec gate and a new
# subprocess there was caught by nothing. The weaker rule over the WHOLE tool, plus a method-level
# declaration, is the stronger gate. A policy is only as strong as the scope it is actually evaluated
# over.
deny Net Db Ipc
